this is your tech news briefing for  thursday september 8th i'm zoe thomas  for the wall street journal  apple is sometimes accused of creating a  walled garden an ecosystem that's  beautiful but locks users into it  for its part apple says it makes systems  that customers want to use and that  integrate well with other products and  services it offers  that was the pitch from ceo tim cook at  the company's big annual event on  wednesday where it revealed new iphones  a new apple smart watch and updated  airpod headphones  they're always with you useful wherever  and whenever you need them and are  designed to work seamlessly together  on their own each is industry leading  together they provide a magical  experience  to help convince users of their magic  apple also made a surprise move it's  keeping prices steady  joining us to discuss the company's  approach and the new features it's  bringing to its products is our personal  tech bureau chief wilson rothman hi  wilson hey zoe how are you i'm great so  this is the event where apple unveils  its new line of iphones what do we get  with the new iphone 14 well i think that  one of the big things they did was they  brought back a very popular phone from  about five years ago that plus sized  phone that so many people had the seven  plus or the eight plus and then they  kind of did away with that nice value it  was like a big screen but for a decent  price  so what you have now is the 14 plus  which is an 899 phone  that has kind of the lower specs of the  14  but the bigger screen of those pro max  phones that have cost 1100 bucks all  right so let's talk about some of the  differences here because if you were you  know trolling social media as i was  during the event you saw a lot of people  comparing the iphone 14 to  previous generations of iphones so what  is different in these new models yeah  you know the lower two of the iphone 14s  really do resemble the previous phones  in a lot of ways so i don't disagree  with people who say they're almost  indistinguishable except for that one  big size bump because the 13 had a mini  we don't have a mini anymore so that  breaks the heart of some people but  sales wise that's not as big a thing so  the bigger size is what apple was going  for on the iphone 14. now the iphone 14  pro has a lot of new things that are  different and new one of the biggest is  that the camera the top camera in there  is now 48 megapixels that's up from 12.  so it's a big jump for apple that's  always kind of played down the megapixel  game suddenly they have this gigantic  camera in there another important thing  about the iphone 14 pro is that it has  an always on display this is something  that competitors like the samsung phones  have had where basically the screen dims  and you just see the really important  stuff like you know certain widgets or  notifications and then the time you  don't have to like reach over and tap  your phone but it slows down the refresh  rate so that it's not draining the  battery normally the phone can refresh  at like 60 times a second this makes it  refresh only around one time per second  let's talk about the other products that  apple brought out on wednesday because  it wasn't just about the phone and tim  cook kind of made a big deal of this at  the beginning saying it's all about  these products integrating together so  one of the things that they have is a  new version of the apple watch can you  tell us briefly what's new here one of  the the really cool things they added  was a thermometer that can help with uh  there's an ovulation app and a period  tracker that's already in the apple  health kind of stack of programs and  this thermometer will help with that  will give additional useful information  for women who are tracking their periods  in ovulation  another feature that's new to the  regular apple watch is crash detection  where it uses motion sensors inside to  determine whether you've been in  several different types of car accidents  including rolling over and getting hit  on the side but the big news was a big  big big watch  it's gonna look chunky on some people  but it is something that a lot of you  know extreme athletes and people who do  things even like i do i'm not an extreme  athlete but i love diving the new apple  watch ultra it's 800 bucks but it has  all these features that we haven't seen  before it is as i mentioned diving it is  a dive computer it can go down i think  around 130 feet it's extremely rugged  it's got a 36 hour battery and it's got  an optimized battery setting so it can  actually go even longer up to 60 hours  so it's a monster of a watch but it's  kind of what everyone's been waiting for  because  on that high end apple hasn't really  been able to compete with garmin which  is kind of the big time athlete's watch  of choice  the other thing they brought out is a  new generation of their headphones did  much change there  so you know they look the same  they have longer battery life out of the  box so it's a six hour battery life i  think that's a 20 30 percent improvement  it's really it's going to be noticeable  for people the case you know charging  case together provides up to 30 hours of  battery life which is really nice and  they've also improved the noise  cancellation as you might know there's a  transparency mode we call it the don't  get hit by a bus mode like you're  crossing the street you hear a honk you  look up you know if you have full noise  cancelling on you won't hear that honk  but if you push it into transparency  mode you will so now they have something  called adaptive transparency mode which  i think has more like it's using more of  the sensors that make noise canceling  work  but it's doing more to like kind of  figure out maybe make it even easier to  hear that bus coming or that hear that  honking of a horn you know overall  smartphone sales are down for the  industry people have been pulling back  on buying expensive tech gadgets  altogether and apple has seemed to you  know defy that trend but it did decide  not to raise prices on iphone models  what does this tell us about apple's  strategy i think that shows a conscious  decision on the bean counters at apple  on their part to like  not shock people with price maybe this  inflation thing is scaring enough people  and suddenly you hear oh the price isn't  going up on this  wow that's great news it's almost like a  discount in an inflationary environment  i mean that i do think that's a  calculation a marketing calculation that  they're making a bet that they're going  to sell more because of that to offset  whatever cost they're eating by not  raising the price  the other thing is  that the premium tier  of smartphones the 900 and up have  actually not  experienced any sales depression the way  these lower end phones have  overall you find people are willing to  hold on to their phones longer  but the people spending less money on  their phones are actually more likely to  hold on to them longer because they  don't care  people like me i'm a nerd i like my  phones you know i spent a thousand  dollars on it you know last year i'm  probably gonna sit out this year but you  bet next year i'm gonna buy a new phone  i think it's justifiable for people who  are you know have a lot going on in  their phones that they want to have  something that works and keeps up with  the times  but it is true that there's a certain  sales bracket that actually hasn't had a  decline and that's where apple plays  mostly in that sandbox  all right that's our personal tech  editor wilson rothman thanks for joining  us wilson it's my pleasure though  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing  for more tech stories check out our  website wsj.com i'm zoe thomas for the  wall street journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

